**Interviewer:** "What are governor limits and how did your code handle them?"

**You:** *"Governor limits prevent any single process from hogging shared resources on Salesforce's multi-tenant platform. The most common ones are 100 SOQL queries and 150 DML statements per transaction. We handled it through bulkification — collecting records into lists first, doing one SOQL query outside the loop, then processing everything in bulk. In the Lead trigger for example, we build the WHERE clause lists first, run one query for all matching Accounts, then loop through results."*

---

**Interviewer:** "Speaking of SOQL — what's the difference between SOQL and SOSL?"

**You:** *"SOQL is Salesforce Object Query Language — it queries a specific object, like SELECT Id, Name FROM Account. SOSL is Salesforce Object Search Language — it searches across multiple objects at once using a text search index. SOSL is better when you don't know which object the data lives in. SOQL is more precise and used more commonly in triggers and logic."*

---

**Interviewer:** "What happens if you put a SOQL query inside a for loop?"

**You:** *"That's a governor limit violation waiting to happen. Every iteration of the loop fires a separate SOQL query, and you only have 100 per transaction. If you're processing 200 records in a bulk trigger, you'd hit the limit immediately and get a LimitException. The fix is to collect all the values you need first, then run one SOQL query outside the loop using an IN clause."*

