**Interviewer:** "What's the difference between a workflow rule and a Flow?"

**You:** *"Workflow rules are the older, simpler automation tool — they can do basic things like field updates, email alerts, and outbound messages, but only on a single object and only when a record is created or edited. Flows are much more powerful — they can work across multiple objects, handle complex logic, create and update related records, interact with users through screen flows, and even call Apex. Salesforce has been retiring workflow rules in favor of Flows, so Flow is now the go-to declarative automation tool."*

---

**Interviewer:** "When would you use a Flow over Apex?"

**You:** *"If the business requirement can be handled declaratively — no complex calculations, no bulk processing, no external callouts — Flow is preferred because it's faster to build and admins can maintain it without code. Apex is better when you need bulk processing, complex logic like our fiscal year sales calculator, integrations with external systems, or when you need precise control over governor limits."*

