**Interviewer:** "What is a Lightning Web Component and have you worked with them?"

**You:** ⚠️ *Know this:*

*"Lightning Web Components, or LWC, is Salesforce's modern framework for building UI components using standard web technologies — HTML, JavaScript, and CSS. They're faster and more lightweight than the older Aura framework. I have exposure to LWC from my time at Ridgelinez — our project had an lwc folder with components. LWC components communicate with each other through properties using @api for parent-to-child, and custom events for child-to-parent. They can call Apex methods using @wire for reactive data or imperative calls for on-demand data fetching."*

---

**Interviewer:** "What's the difference between @wire and an imperative Apex call in LWC?"

**You:** ⚠️ *Know this:*

*"@wire is reactive — it automatically calls the Apex method when the component loads or when its parameters change, and re-renders when the data updates. It's good for read-only data that you want to stay in sync. An imperative call is manual — you call the Apex method explicitly in JavaScript, like inside a button click handler. It's better when you need to control exactly when the call happens, like on a form submit, or when you need to handle errors more specifically."*

